[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Blocks, Procs, and Lambdas in Ruby on Rails"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Ruby and Ruby on Rails, closures are a fundamental concept. They allow you to encapsulate logic, pass behavior as arguments, and write DRY, highly expressive code. While blocks, procs, and lambdas share similarities, they have distinct differences in syntax, return behavior, and argument checking."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Blocks"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A block is just a chunk of code enclosed between do...end or curly braces {}. Blocks are not objects in Ruby, which is their primary distinction from procs and lambdas. They cannot be saved to a variable or passed around independently unless converted."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"[1, 2, 3].each do |number|
  puts number * 2
end"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Rails, blocks are heavily used in ActiveRecord scopes, iterators, and routing configurations. For example, defining a database migration relies entirely on blocks."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"create_table :users do |t|
  t.string :name
  t.timestamps
end"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Procs"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Proc is an instantiation of the Proc class, which means it is an object. Because it is an object, you can assign it to a variable, pass it to methods, and store it in data structures. Procs are lenient with arguments and use 'return' to exit the surrounding method."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"my_proc = Proc.new { |x| puts "Value: #{x}" }
my_proc.call(10)"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Rails, Procs are frequently used in model validations or callbacks where conditional logic needs to be evaluated dynamically at runtime."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"class User < ApplicationRecord
  before_save :update_status, if: Proc.new { |user| user.age > 18 }
end"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Lambdas"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"A lambda is a special type of Proc. It differs from a standard Proc in two major ways: strict argument checking (it raises an ArgumentError if the wrong number of arguments is passed) and return behavior (it returns from itself, not the surrounding method)."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"my_lambda = ->(x) { x * 2 }
my_lambda.call(5) # Returns 10"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Rails, lambdas are the preferred choice for defining ActiveRecord scopes because they are lazy-evaluated and enforce strict parameter boundaries."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"class Article < ApplicationRecord
  scope :published, -> { where(published: true) }
  scope :recent, ->(days) { where('created_at >= ?', days.days.ago) }
end"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Differences"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Blocks: Not objects, syntax is do...end or {}, lenient arguments, returns from surrounding method."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Procs: Instances of Proc, lenient arguments, returns from surrounding method."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lambdas: Instances of Proc (behaves like a method), strict arguments, returns from itself only."}]
